# Image-to-3D Visual Decomposition Rules

## 1. Geometric Decomposition (Anti-Primitive Rule)
- Never generate a standalone primitive (box, sphere, cylinder) as a final component.
- Always decompose reference shapes into a clustered compound hierarchy of intersecting primitives.
- Use `ProBoolean` compound objects programmatically to fuse (`Union`), subtract (`Sub`), or intersect shapes to match the complex silhouettes found in the reference image.

## 2. Procedural Sculpting & Volumetric Blending
- **Voxel/Volume Merging:** To mimic organic or organic-mechanical sculpting, bridge primitive joints by converting compound shapes into a unified volume. Apply a `Vol. Select` or `VertexPaint` modifier combined with soft selection to smooth transitions.
- **Sub-D Sculpting:** Convert base composite meshes to `Editable Poly`, run random localized face subdivisions, and use `polyOp.moveVerts` or structural noise modifiers (`SpaceWarp` or `Noise` modifier with low scale/high strength) to sculpt high-poly surface organic imperfections.
- **Displacement Maps:** Utilize mathematical cellular, perlin, or wood noise maps assigned to a high-density `Displace` modifier to procedurally simulate complex panel greebles or chiseled sci-fi indentations without writing thousands of lines of individual poly coordinates.

## 3. Surface Carving & Silhouette Matching
- When parsing an image description, extract the major silhouette (Silhouette A) and minor cutout details (Cutouts B, C).
- Script automated slice planes (`polyOp.slice`) or boolean cutting matrices to carve intricate paths, panel splits, and wire channels directly into the high-poly blended forms.
